{:edb/db-before-t 2855 :edb/db-after-t 2856 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536168 50 #inst "2026-10-09T17:55:06.860Z" 13194139536168 true] [17592186082925 1100 "Compute the arc length of  the curve $y=x^{3/2}$ between the points $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/assets/4ed8b94a4fff3c22697264e467f955f2e51a62618d03295c4e4f38493bff2ced.png)" 13194139536168 false] [17592186082925 1100 "Compute the arc length of  the curve $y=x^{3/2}$ between the points $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/assets/4ed8b94a4fff3c22697264e467f955f2e51a62618d03295c4e4f38493bff2ced.png)" 13194139536168 true] [17592186083120 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386956 13194139536168 true] [17592186083120 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386959 13194139536168 true] [17592186083120 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386962 13194139536168 true] [17592186083122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386942 13194139536168 true] [17592186083122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386986 13194139536168 true] [17592186083122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386993 13194139536168 true] [17592186083213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386949 13194139536168 true] [17592186083213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386965 13194139536168 true] [17592186083213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386972 13194139536168 true] [17592186083213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386979 13194139536168 true] [17592186083860 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536168 true] [17592186083860 1100 "Compute the arc length of $y = \\frac{1}{2}(e^{x} + e^{-x})$ for $x ∈ [0, \\ln 2]$." 13194139536168 false] [17592186083860 1100 "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})$ for $x\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2].$" 13194139536168 true] [17592186083860 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}),$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2x}-2+{e}^{-2x})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2x}+2+{e}^{-2x})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}{({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}){|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})-({e}^{0}-{e}^{-0})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[2-\\frac{1}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386942 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386943 13194139536168 true] [17592186386942 1089 "q-122391" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386942 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536168 true] [17592186386942 1100 "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{3-2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386942 1113 #uuid "e9f04453-3f40-58db-b58f-2274524d2d22" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386942 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{3-2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{3-2{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=-\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386943 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386944 13194139536168 true] [17592186386943 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386945 13194139536168 true] [17592186386943 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386946 13194139536168 true] [17592186386943 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386947 13194139536168 true] [17592186386943 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386948 13194139536168 true] [17592186386943 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536168 true] [17592186386943 1041 "selection" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386943 1104 #edb/ref 17592186386944 13194139536168 true] [17592186386943 1110 #uuid "38800ebd-2861-5022-8a8b-fee3be9419b9" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386944 1034 "y=-\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{2}" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386944 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536168 true] [17592186386944 1111 #uuid "406028df-0352-560b-9b42-264aa756c1a6" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386945 1034 "y=-\\sqrt{3-2x}+\\sqrt{2}" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386945 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536168 true] [17592186386945 1111 #uuid "47a372f6-4233-5266-93f3-ef8753a1eb85" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386946 1034 "y=-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{2}" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386946 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536168 true] [17592186386946 1111 #uuid "1534a69f-f3d9-5b03-8edb-768a987593bd" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386947 1034 "y=-\\sqrt{2-{x}^{3}}+\\sqrt{2}" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386947 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536168 true] [17592186386947 1111 #uuid "cd1aa5b0-9c65-56ac-bb38-4174d31bdf2f" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386948 1034 "y=-\\sqrt{3+{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{2}" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386948 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536168 true] [17592186386948 1111 #uuid "2e775025-e28f-5a75-b066-0119bf43609d" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386949 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386950 13194139536168 true] [17592186386949 1089 "q-15405" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386949 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536168 true] [17592186386949 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/assets/f74f28bf0b5bf2208b3cebf837c667cf9a9ae737d7eb758708a98548ffca8e3a.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y=4{sin}^{2}⁡x$ on $[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ as shown above?" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386949 1113 #uuid "898e2d00-d1c0-5287-b1c1-c8753234b0c5" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386949 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=4{sin}^{2}⁡x,$ $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386951 13194139536168 true] [17592186386950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386952 13194139536168 true] [17592186386950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386953 13194139536168 true] [17592186386950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386954 13194139536168 true] [17592186386950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386955 13194139536168 true] [17592186386950 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536168 true] [17592186386950 1041 "selection" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386950 1104 #edb/ref 17592186386952 1319413953616