{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{8},$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{e}^{8x}-2+{e}^{-8x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{8x}+2+{e}^{-8x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x})^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{8})|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{4(1/2)}-{e}^{-4(1/2)}}{8}]-[\\frac{{e}^{4(0)}-{e}^{-4(0)}}{8}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}({e}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{8{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386957 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386958 13194139536168 true] [17592186386957 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536168 true] [17592186386957 1041 "field-1" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386957 1104 #edb/ref 17592186386958 13194139536168 true] [17592186386957 1110 #uuid "7839e8fd-b1aa-5a55-8b22-9ada1841643a" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386958 1034 "\\frac{e^{2}-e^{-2}}{8}" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386958 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536168 true] [17592186386958 1111 #uuid "1916daff-8ec2-549c-89d4-471a53de781f" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386959 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386960 13194139536168 true] [17592186386959 1089 "q-353191" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386959 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536168 true] [17592186386959 1100 "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)$ for $x\\in [1,3].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386959 1113 #uuid "e41c732f-7225-5d09-880d-7d7b833ee64e" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386959 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x),$ $a=1,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}+16}{16{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}}{16{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{3}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]-[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386960 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386961 13194139536168 true] [17592186386960 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536168 true] [17592186386960 1041 "field-1" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386960 1104 #edb/ref 17592186386961 13194139536168 true] [17592186386960 1110 #uuid "2f625505-43f9-5735-96aa-6a3eb36b033f" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386961 1034 "1+\\ln(3)" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386961 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536168 true] [17592186386961 1111 #uuid "1a267970-fb52-5c96-bbf8-30c567eef7db" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386962 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386963 13194139536168 true] [17592186386962 1089 "q-362543" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386962 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536168 true] [17592186386962 1100 "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [3,6].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386962 1113 #uuid "b4767432-922a-5be8-be5b-18e91bf8210c" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386962 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)^{3/2},$ $a=3,$ and $b=6.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(x-3)^{1/2}=\\sqrt{x-3}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\sqrt{x-3}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\sqrt{x-2}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}\\sqrt{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{3}(x-2)^{3/2})|}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{2}{3}(6-2)^{3/2}]-[\\frac{2}{3}(3-2)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386963 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386964 13194139536168 true] [17592186386963 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536168 true] [17592186386963 1041 "field-1" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386963 1104 #edb/ref 17592186386964 13194139536168 true] [17592186386963 1110 #uuid "25b10528-df5c-5479-b078-dad4157f08bb" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386964 1034 "\\frac{14}{3}" 13194139536168 true] [17592186386964 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536168 true] [17592186386964 1111 #uuid "39ca2ec5-8a34-50d2-a165-7227d